Well, only because Byron forgot a crucial step;
dropping the money acquired by selling stuff, after
each sales sweep, turns that cash into additional
credit and creates an ever-growing pile of gold on
the floor at your feet. Just make sure, when you
pick up stuff to buy and sell it again, that you
DON'T PICK UP THE GOLD, or you're about to hand it
back to the shopkeeper, a really frustrating error!

However, sales back to the shopkeeper are at a very
sharp discount, depending on the PCs charisma, so
eventually you _do_ run out of money, as Byron said,
now go on to:

>> 5. Have pet steal money from floor and drop it
>> outside the shop.

Which carries out (possibly lots) more money than
the PC carried in, which is where the gain occurs.

> Too late. You have to do that _before_ you convert
> your credit to money (using steps 1-3 above),

Well, no, you just need to get step 3.5 in there
where it belongs.

> because that process has negative returns. So you
> have to do it with cloned credit to get something
> out of it.

Sure, but when you go in and drop your cash, you've
GOT credit. Buying and immediately selling stuff and
then DROPPING THE CASH creates more credit, just not
as much with each round of pick stuff up, pay for
(part of) it, drop it to sell it back, drop the cash
that dropping and selling stuff acquired to create
more credit.

When pooch/furball carries that cash out and you
carry it back in and drop it, you've cloned _more_
credit than you got the first time, which speeds
stuff up compared to just hauling the initial cash
in and out lots of times.

